---
import ComparisonTable from "~/components/illustration-kit/ComparisonTable.astro"
import Disclosure from "~/components/illustration-kit/Disclosure.astro"
import IllustrationFigure from "~/components/illustration-kit/IllustrationFigure.astro"
import Panel from "~/components/illustration-kit/Panel.astro"
---

<IllustrationFigure
  name="discursive-dilemma"
  frame={false}
  lead="Three judges, one disagreement"
>
  <ComparisonTable
    caption="What each judge thinks"
    columns={["Judge", "Valid?", "Broken?", "Liable?"]}
    rows={[
      ["A", "Yes", "No", "No"],
      ["B", "No", "Yes", "No"],
      ["C", "Yes", "Yes", "Yes"],
    ]}
    align="center"
    dense
  />
  <Disclosure summary="Count the majorities">
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 pt-3">
      <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <Panel title="Vote on the reasons" class="flex flex-col gap-3">
          <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
            <p>Valid: <strong>2 of 3 judges say yes</strong></p>
            <p>Broken: <strong>2 of 3 judges say yes</strong></p>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-auto border-t border-ink pt-3 text-2xl font-bold">Result: Liable</p>
        </Panel>
        <Panel title="Vote on the verdict" class="flex flex-col gap-3">
          <p>Liable: <strong>2 of 3 judges say no</strong></p>
          <p class="mt-auto border-t border-ink pt-3 text-2xl font-bold">Result: Not liable</p>
        </Panel>
      </div>
      <p class="text-base leading-normal">
        Both methods use majority voting, but they reach opposite verdicts.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Disclosure>
  <Fragment slot="caption">
    Each judge's verdict follows from their own answers. The disagreement arises when the
    court must choose whether to vote on the reasons or on the final verdict.
  </Fragment>
</IllustrationFigure>
